  <problem>
    <statement>
      Let X be a Riemann Surface with genus g, and let S^g(X) be the symmetric power of X,
      identified with the set of effective divisors of degree g. Consider the Abel-Jacobi map
      A: S^g(X) -> Jac(X). For any D in S^g(X), A^{-1}A(D) is given by the linear system |D|.
      Given that A has constant rank along A^{-1}A(D), does this imply that A^{-1}A(D) is a
      submanifold of S^g(X)?
    </statement>
    <correct_answer>No. Constant rank along a fiber (i.e., the rank being the same at every point of the fiber) does NOT in general imply that the fiber is a submanifold. The Constant Rank Theorem requires constant rank in a neighborhood of the fiber, which is a strictly stronger condition. A counterexample is f(x,y) = (x^2 + y^3)^2, which has constant rank 0 along the fiber f^{-1}(0) = {x^2 + y^3 = 0} (a cusp curve), but this cusp is not a smooth submanifold.</correct_answer>
  </problem>

